Z||000000||Firebug||02-20-2001||01:18 AM||||I am currently putting my fiero back on the road after 6 years.  I am putting a 2.8L in place of it's 2.5.  I am undecided yet about converting it to a 3.1.  As far as wiring goes, I have a harness from a '85 fiero V-6 for the interior and also the engine harness from the same car.  Am I missing anything wiring wise?  The car is an '85.  I'm hoping the current guages will work for the time being as I am working on trying to make some of the fiero shows this summer.  One other problem I'm encountering is that the harnesses I have are for a V-6 fiero with Auto trans and AC.  My fiero is one of the rare ones that didn't come with AC.  Will I have any problems by simply not hooking up an AC system to the harness?  <P>Thank you for your responses as I'm kinda new here...<P>Scott||||reg||1
Z||000001||pontiacs4me||02-20-2001||03:00 AM||||Im doing practically the same thing you are.  I have 2 fieros, a 85 se with the 2.8 liter and a 87 with the 2.5.  I am taking the 2.8 out of the 85 and putting it in the 87. I need to use the wiring harness and ecm out of the 85 to get it all to work.  I pulled out the 2.5 today and there is this plug that has about 6 wires that plugs into where the shifter linkage goes to the transaxle (automatic by the way). Well the engine is coming out of a manual car. Is the ecm's different?  Will I have to take the plug off the 2.5 liter harness and splice it into the 2.8 liter harness to get whatever it runs to work?  I dont even know what it does. <BR>And I decided to leave the ac parts off.  I have never owned an ac car, and Id rather have the extra hp.  Can I get a belt that will be the perfect legnth to bypass it?<BR>I figured Id post under this topic unstead of starting a new one since we are basically doing the same thing.  thanks in advance.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Carrolles||02-20-2001||07:23 AM||||The v-6 A/C compressor is powered by its own belt.  No need to get a longer one for the alternator, etc.<P>My A/C compressor is without a belt on my 85GT.  I don't see how not having it hooked up to the wiring harness would cause any problems.  The ECM does interface the fuel dilivery with the engaging of the A/C clutch.  It gives a little burp to the gas about 1/2 second before it kicks in the A/C clutch.<P>If I remember correctly, the wires that go to the auto trans on the back side control the rear hatch release lockout (does not allow the electric rear hatch release operate if the engine is in gear), back-up lights, and I don't remember what else.  Theogre probably knows.<P>If you are sure you'll never need the A/C compressor on the v-6 you might consider leaving the A/C compressor support bracket off to give better access the the front exhaust manifold in the future.  You'll need to replace the three bolts that hold the bracket on with shorter ones.  These bolts also hold the dogbone strut brace (and maybe the oil pressure sending unit, not sure).<P>Good luck,  <P>Carroll||||reg||1
